Build Python package inits from /-separated names on Windows, as everything else in writer.go does.

AddInitPyFiles walked member names with filepath.Dir and filepath.Base. Zip member names are
always /-separated; on Windows those functions split on the backslash as well and hand back the
host form, so a member a/b/c.py gave the directory a\b. Its __init__.py was looked up as
a\b/__init__.py, never found, and written empty - beside the real a/b/__init__.py. zipimport
normalises separators, so the empty one is what loads: every python_test built on Windows failed
with "module 'xmlrunner' has no attribute 'XMLTestRunner'", because the test runner package came
out with nothing in it. shouldInclude matched patterns with filepath.Match for the same names, and
on Windows that function reads a backslash in a pattern as a separator rather than an escape.

Applies after arcat_windows_rename.patch. See docs/design/windows/07-state-of-play.md.

